Intro
-----

This little lot started out as a brief exercise to try out a few techniques,
but, as so often happens, it starts to take over your life. So, there are
many additional things that could be done to improve it, but, I retire in
30 or so years and I'd like to try something else before I reach that age, so
I'm releasing it as is, and will more than likely keep adding to it when the
mood comes.

The program basically uses two database files. One holds info on all the
films Laurel and Hardy have made and the other holds details on the various
'tents' around the world.

The film database can be edited by using the built-in Psion database
application, or within the program itself. See the Menu for various Help
topics. The tents database will tell you the nearest tent in your area.
This last, inocuous sounding function caused the most grief. I wanted to add
scrolling dialog boxes to guide the user to the nearest town. Upon scouring
the programming manual, I find no reference at all to scrolling dialog
boxes! Fortunately, a colleague had written a suite of routines to handle
such events (Paul Cooke, who gave his kind permission to use the routines in
this program). After much, much re-working, I finally got it to look
something like I wanted it to.

The program also loads a converted GIF image to use as a base screen. It does
look rather cute seeing digitised images on a tiny hand held machine. Is'nt
modern technology wonderful?


Anyway, to get the thing running on your machine:-

    Place L&H3.APP into your APP directory
    Place Tents.dat and L&H2.dbf into your DAT directory
    Place L&H.pic into your PIC directory and
    Place L&H1.pic into your PIC/BITMAPS directory

Install L&H3.APP in the usual way, and press enter on the bowler hats!

